<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gabo Reforms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gabo Reforms were a programme of reforms carried out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from 1894 to 1896 by a pro-Japanese government led by Kim Hong-jip, after Japanese troops occupied the royal palace. They abolished the legal distinction between yangban and commoners, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, the civil service examinations and child marriage, and reorganised government and finance on modern lines. Imposed under Japanese pressure, they were resented by many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a>, and some were reversed after 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gabo-reforms.jpg" alt="Gabo Reforms" width="282" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nuyos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gungukgimucheo_joseokjin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
